WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.517
Imagine you have thirty dollars in your pocket, and you owe a friend twenty dollars by Friday. Can you pay? Yes, with some to spare.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:26.296
Divide what you have by what you owe. Thirty divided by twenty is one point five. You have one dollar fifty for every one dollar you owe. We write this as one point five to one.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:45.991
A business does the same check. Current assets are things it owns that will turn into cash within a year: cash, inventory, and money owed by customers. Current liabilities are debts it must pay within a year, such as money owed to suppliers or an overdraft.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:01:00.324
The current ratio is current assets divided by current liabilities. The formula is given in the exam, but you must put the right figures in the right places. Assets go on top.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:16.722
Step one, find both figures for the year the question asks about. Step two, divide. You do not take anything away. Step three, round as the question says, usually to two decimal places, and write it against one.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:27.918
What does the answer mean? Above one, current assets are bigger than current liabilities, so they could cover the short-term debts. Below one, they could not.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:48.776
Here is a real question from Paper one, June twenty twenty-three, about a chocolate shop. Its current assets are twenty-six thousand euros and its current liabilities are fourteen thousand eight hundred and fifty-two euros. Calculate the current ratio to two decimal places. It is worth two marks.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:55.045
Set up the formula with the figures. Twenty-six thousand on top, fourteen thousand eight hundred and fifty-two underneath.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:06.922
Divide. The calculator shows one point seven five zero six and more. To two decimal places, that is one point seven five, or one point seven five to one.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.483
So the shop has one euro seventy-five of current assets for every one euro it owes in the short term. That is above one.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:26.847
The most common mistake is turning the ratio upside down: liabilities divided by assets. That gives zero point five seven, which is wrong.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:38.605
Another mistake is taking inventory away first. That gives the acid test ratio, a different calculation. For the current ratio, use all the current assets.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:56.694
Now you try. A café has current assets of nine thousand three hundred pounds and current liabilities of four thousand two hundred pounds. Calculate its current ratio to two decimal places. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:12.873
Nine thousand three hundred divided by four thousand two hundred is two point two one four and more. To two decimal places, two point two one, or two point two one to one. The café has more than twice what it owes in the short term.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:24.426
Remember the method: find the two figures, divide assets by liabilities, then round and write it against one. Now try the practice questions for this type.
